all have in-degree 1, the first cycle begins from a (the last node), producing a → b → a, which reports a, b correctly. After breaking this cycle, the remaining nodes are [d, c].\n\nFrom c (the last remaining node), the next cycle is c → d → c, reporting c, d. This confirms the cycle walk starts from the first-encountered remaining node, which is the last one in the prepended list.\n\nNow I need to figure out which successor to follow when a node has multiple options. Let me test `a b a c b a`: the edges are a→b, a→c, b→a, giving in-degrees of a=1, b=1, c=1. All nodes have in-degree > 0, so a cycle exists.\n\nStarting from a, I can follow either b or c as successors—following b gives a→b→a (cycle: a, b), while following c leads nowhere since c has no outgoing edges. Either way, the cycle is a, b. Rather than getting bogged down in which successor to prioritize, I should just implement the algorithm and test it against gtsort. The core approach is maintaining nodes in reverse-encounter order, scanning for zero-in-degree nodes to output, decrementing their successors' in-degrees, and repeating until stuck.\n\nWhen stuck, I walk from the first-encountered remaining node by following its first active successor (one still in the graph) until I revisit a node, which marks the cycle. I report the cycle, break it by removing an edge, and continue the topological sort. The assumption is that every remaining node eventually reaches a cycle since the graph only contains those nodes.\n\nBut wait—remaining nodes have in-degree > 0, not necessarily successors. A node like b might have predecessors but no outgoing edges, so the walk can't pass through it. I think the algorithm just skips nodes with no active successors and only walks from nodes that can actually lead somewhere.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The cycle detection seems to work by picking the first remaining node, following its successor chain until revisiting a node, then reporting that cycle. For `a b b c 