ueue, and if anything remains, run cycle detection via in-order traversal to identify and report the problematic loop before continuing.\n\nThe tricky part is the `detect_loop` function—it walks nodes in order, skipping those already processed, marks the first unprocessed node with positive count as the loop start, then checks subsequent nodes to see if any of their successors point back to that start node, and if the qlink chain is properly set, I can confirm the complete cycle.\n\nImplementing the retrace logic... I'm realizing the second while loop handles any remaining items in the qlink chain that weren't part of the cycle itself—after the first loop breaks at k and cleans up the cycle, there might still be predecessor nodes pointing into it that need their qlinks cleared out. I see—after the break, the second while loop continues from where k is, clearing k's qlink pointer and then following the rest of the chain to clean up all the remaining pointers. Let me trace through the cycle detection again: starting from the head of the detected cycle, we print each node and set its qlink to NULL as we traverse, until we hit k itself where we break without clearing it yet. Then the second loop picks up from k and finishes the cleanup by clearing k's qlink and following through to the end of the chain. After the cycle is broken and the node count drops to zero, the main loop returns to scan_zeros to find and process those newly freed nodes. I also need to handle the error message formatting—GNU's `error(0, 0, ...)` prints to stderr with the program name prepended, so I'll replicate that behavior in my Go implementation. I'm checking what the actual output looks like when gtsort processes these cases—whether filenames get quoted and how the error messages are formatted across different scenarios. Looking at the actual test output more carefully, there are no quotes around the filenames in the error messages, so I should match that format exactly in my Go implementation 